# M_PwModelExample_VertexColor - the material the .pwmodel examples expect.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every geometry op in a .pwmodel accepts color=(r,g,b,a),
# and the compiler writes it into the static mesh's FColorVertexBuffer. Nothing
# in the engine's stock material set samples that buffer, so an example bound to
# BasicShapeMaterial renders every op the same grey and the colour is data on
# disk that no one can see. This material is the missing half: VertexColor into
# BaseColor.
#
# HOW TO INSTALL IT. Compile this text into your project - the entry path below
# is where the examples expect to find it:
#
#   call("material.compile_mgir", { text: <this file>, save: true })
#   call("material.authoring.compile_material",
#        { assetPath: "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_VertexColor" })
#
# Nothing breaks if you skip it. A slot bound to a path that will not load
# degrades to the default material with a named PWMODEL_STAGE_WARNING and the
# asset is still written.
#
# THE SLOT/COLOUR SPLIT. A material slot is for a different SHADING BEHAVIOUR -
# iron against gasket, clay against wick. Vertex colour is for tint variation
# WITHIN one slot. Roughness/Metallic/Specular are scalar parameters here so one
# graph serves every surface family through material instances; see
# model.vertex-color.md in the wiki for the instance table.

entry material `/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/M_PwModelExample_VertexColor` {
    %vertexColor = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionVertexColor`() @(-600, -100)
    %roughness = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Roughness", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 32, DefaultValue: 0.6) @(-600, 120)
    %metallic = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Metallic", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 32, DefaultValue: 0.0) @(-600, 260)
    %specular = call `/Script/Engine.MaterialExpressionScalarParameter`(ParameterName: "Specular", Group: "Surface", SortPriority: 32, DefaultValue: 0.5) @(-600, 400)

    output BaseColor: %vertexColor
    output Roughness: %roughness
    output Metallic: %metallic
    output Specular: %specular
}
